 1 big thing: DOJ sues to delay Alaska results 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Jim Watson/AFP via Getty Images The Trump administration sued Alaska today to delay certification of next month's Senate election results if incumbent Republican Sen. Dan Sullivan narrowly loses, Axios' Avery Lotz reports. 

 The race between incumbent Sullivan, Democrat Mary Peltola and retired teacher Dan Sullivan — which could determine Senate control — has been marked by controversy. 🤔 Republicans accuse Democrats of trying to confuse voters by planting a candidate with the same name as the incumbent.

 The retired teacher Sullivan will appear on the ballot as Daniel J. Sullivan Jr. Alaska's use of ranked-choice voting complicates matters, eliminating candidates in rounds if no one gets 50% of the initial vote. The lawsuit , filed in Alaska District Court, says: "An election result may be impacted by voter confusion if: (1) Senator Sullivan does not win the election; and (2) had all votes cast for Daniel J. Sullivan been cast instead for Senator Sullivan and Alaska's ranked-choice process played out accordingly, Senator Sullivan would have won the election."

 It requests a delay in certification until after an investigation determines "whether votes cast for Daniel J. Sullivan were meant to be cast for Senator Sullivan." President Trump personally ordered the lawsuit to be filed, AP reports . 🗳️ The big picture: The Trump administration is pushing for greater control of state-run elections and casting doubt ahead of time on results that may favor Democrats.

 ✅ Reality check: Notre Dame law professor Derek Muller tells AP that the Alaska suit won't survive long, since the statute "simply doesn't let the Department of Justice enjoin certification."

